  "live_records": {
    "q-253549": {
      "dom_id": "step-q253549",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Consider the set $A,$ defined as\n\n$$\nA=\\{\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}+1}:n\\in \\{1,2,3,4\\}\\}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the supremum of $A?$\n\n$sup(A)=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "The supremum of a set $A$ is the smallest upper bound of $A.$\n\nIf $A$ is a finite set, the supremum of $A$ equals its maximum. \n\nFirst, let's write down all the terms of our set by substituting $n=1,2,3,4$ into the expression for the elements of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(-1)^{1}}{(1)^{2}+1} & =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{(-1)^{2}}{(2)^{2}+1} & =\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ \\frac{(-1)^{3}}{(3)^{2}+1} & =-\\frac{1}{10} \\\\ \\frac{(-1)^{4}}{(4)^{2}+1} & =\\frac{1}{17}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nA=\\{-\\frac{1}{2},\\,\\frac{1}{5},\\,-\\frac{1}{10},\\,\\frac{1}{17}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sup(A) & =max\\{-\\frac{1}{2},\\,\\frac{1}{5},\\,-\\frac{1}{10},\\,\\frac{1}{17}\\}=\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
