    "q-253526": {
      "dom_id": "step-q253526",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Consider the following set.\n\n$$\nA=\\{7-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{n}\\,:\\,n\\in N\\}\n$$\n\nFor this set, we have {{field-1}}.",
      "worked_solution": "The supremum of a set $A$ is the smallest upper bound of $A.$\n\nTo find the supremum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** upper bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of upper bounds) and take the minimum of this set.\n\nThe elements of $A$ are all the terms of the sequence ${a}_{n},$ given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=7-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{n},\\,n\\in N=\\{1,2,3,4,…\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's draw some points of our sequence on the real line:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14164583/assets/cae6f56db55b49b98df6ce7e3ae0777e725af14de9dedb5b61b54039bac41860.png)\n\nWe see that ${a}_{n}$ is increasing and converges to $7.$ \n\nSo, in our case, we have that\n\n- any number in the set $x\\ge 7$ is an upper bound of $A,$\n\n- any number in the set $x<7$ is **not** an upper bound of $A.$ In other words, we can always find a sufficiently large $n$ so that $7-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{n}$ is greater than $x$ for any $x<7.$\n\nTherefore, $[7,\\infty )$ is the set of upper bounds of $A,$ and \n\n$$\nsup(A)=min[7,\\infty )=7.\n$$\n\nNotice that $7$ is *not* the maximum of $A$ (the set $A$ has no maximum). Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nsup(A)=7\\notin A.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
