    "q-253843": {
      "dom_id": "step-q253843",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "Consider the following set.\n\n$$\nA=\\{3+2{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{n}\\,:\\,n\\in N\\}\n$$\n\nFor this set, we have that {{field-1}}.",
      "worked_solution": "The supremum of a set $A$ is the smallest upper bound of $A.$\n\nTo find the supremum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** upper bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of upper bounds) and take the minimum of this set.\n\nThe elements of $A$ are the terms of the sequence ${a}_{n},$ given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=3+2{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{n},\\,n\\in N=\\{1,2,3,4,…\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's draw some points of our sequence on the real line:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14164583/assets/05737c6e8e7b88f1a91a5fd6cc7d5b6a088227e327ef66e937e279c090a47256.png)\n\nWe see that ${a}_{n}$ is decreasing and converges to $3.$ The maximum of $A$ is \n\n$$\nmax(A)={a}_{1}=3+2{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{1}=\\frac{21}{5}.\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have that\n\n- any number $x\\ge {a}_{1}$ is an upper bound of $A,$\n\n- any number $x<{a}_{1}$ cannot be an upper bound because ${a}_{1},$ an element of $A,$ is greater than $x.$\n\nTherefore, $[\\frac{21}{5},\\infty )$ is the set of upper bounds of $A,$ and \n\n$$\nsup(A)=min[\\frac{21}{5},\\infty )=\\frac{21}{5}.\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\frac{21}{5}$ is the maximum of $A.$ Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nsup(A)=\\frac{21}{5}\\in A.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
