    "q-253897": {
      "dom_id": "step-q253897",
      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14164583/assets/64131e649a82e06d2750d53bdf05b1701b0674a05b70149081ddb58d700adb54.png)\n\nWhat is the supremum of the function $y=f(x)$ shown above?\n\n${sup}_{x\\in R}f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "The supremum of a function is the supremum of the range of the function.\n\nIn this case, the range of $f$ is $A=(-1,2).$\n\nTo find the supremum of a set $A,$ we must find the set containing **all** upper bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of upper bounds) and take the minimum of this set.\n\nSo, in our case, we have that\n\n- any number $y\\ge 2$ is an upper bound of $A,$\n\n- any number $y<2$ is **not** an upper bound of $A.$ In other words, we can always find an element of $A$ that is greater than $y$ for any $y<2.$\n\nTherefore, $[2,\\infty )$ is the set of lower bounds of $A,$ and\n\n$$\n{sup}_{x\\in R}f(x)=sup(A)=min[2,\\infty )=2.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
